Descartes' method of radical doubt — doubting everything that can be doubted — cannot be applied to the act of doubting itself: to doubt is to think, and to think is to exist.
The argument is performatively self-validating: any attempt to deny 'I think therefore I am' requires a thinker to do the denying, which confirms the conclusion.
Unlike all other knowledge claims, the Cogito requires no external verification — it is certain to the person thinking it even if everything else is an illusion or a dream.
Descartes published the argument in the Meditations on First Philosophy (1641); it has been examined by every major philosopher since and no successful logical refutation has been produced.
